[7/20] GPQA Diamond/Physics Find KE of product particles in, Pi(+) = mu(+) + nu here Pi(+)
We have the decay:
π⁺ → μ⁺ + ν_μ
Given: m_π = 139.6 MeV/c², m_μ = 105.7 MeV/c², m_ν ≈ 0.
The pion is at rest, so total energy is m_π c².

Energy conservation:  
m_π c² = E_μ + E_ν  
Momentum conservation: p_μ = p_ν (since initially zero).

For the neutrino: E_ν = p c (massless).  
For the muon: E_μ² = (p c)² + (m_μ c²)².

Let p be the magnitude. Then:
E_μ = √(p²c² + m_μ²c⁴)  
E_ν = p c

So:
m_π c² = √(p²c² + m_μ²c⁴) + p c

Let’s work in MeV units, setting c=1:
139.6 = √(p² + 105.7²) + p  
Let x = p, then:
139.6 - x = √(x² + 105.7²)
Square:
(139.6 - x)² = x² + 105.7²  
139.6² - 2·139.6·x + x² = x² + 105.7²  
139.6² - 2·139.6·x = 105.7²  
Compute squares:
139.6² = 19488.16  
105.7² = 11172.49  
So:
19488.16 - 2·139.6·x = 11172.49  
2·139.6·x = 19488.16 - 11172.49 = 8315.67  
x = 8315.67 / (2·139.6) = 8315.67 / 279.2 ≈ 29.78 MeV/c

Thus neutrino KE = E_ν = p c = 29.78 MeV ≈ 29.8 MeV.  
Muon KE = E_μ - m_μ = √(p² + m_μ²) - m_μ  
Compute: √(29.78² + 105.7²) = √(886.8 + 11172.49) = √12059.29 ≈ 109.82 MeV  
Then KE_μ = 109.82 - 105.7 = 4.12 MeV.

So KE of μ⁺ = 4.12 MeV, ν = 29.8 MeV → matches choice B.

